#!/usr/bin/env python3
# SPDX-License-Identifier: GPL-2.0-only
"""dev: command-line client for a Quest 2 running the mainline initramfs (telnet shell over USB).

    tools/dev check 'CMD' | -f FILE  offline: say whether CMD (or each line of FILE) would be refused
    tools/dev status                 link + uptime + kernel + battery one-liner
    tools/dev battery [--csv]        battery capacity/temp/status/voltage/current
    tools/dev sh 'CMD' [-t SECS]     run CMD on the headset (root busybox sh), print output
    tools/dev pull REMOTE LOCAL      copy a file off the headset (base64 over telnet)
    tools/dev log [N]                last N lines of the target kernel log (/tmp/kernel.log)
    tools/dev return-android [--kexec-dir DIR]
                                     back to Android. Mainline init: creates /tmp/lvr-return; the
                                     init then does a warm reset (through /proc/lvr_warm_reset when it
                                     reads `armed`) or its watchdog-bite reset. Exit 0 "warm reset
                                     presumed" means the link dropped before any reset state was seen;
                                     the pstore console log after the return confirms it. On the
                                     downstream quest-kexec boot (4.19 kernel) it runs that checkout's
                                     tools/return-android.sh: --kexec-dir DIR or $QUEST_KEXEC_DIR
                                     (needed only then).
    tools/dev s2-up [NAME] [--port P] [--sha SHA256]  fetch rootfs/out/NAME (default stage2-latest) from
                                     tools/serve-usb.py into a 1 GiB tmpfs at /stage2,
                                     verify sha256, extract, rbind /proc /sys /dev /tmp
    tools/dev s2 'CMD' [-t SECS]     run CMD inside the stage-2 rootfs (chroot /stage2 /bin/sh -c)
    tools/dev s2-down                unmount everything under /stage2, then the tmpfs; rmdir.
                                     Refuses while processes run in the chroot. Never rm -rf.
    tools/dev syncboss-txlen         write the fixed value 248 to the syncboss
                                     control/transaction_length. The only spi1.0 sysfs write
                                     tools/dev can send; no argument.
    tools/dev fw-fallback on|off|status
                                     firmware-loader sysfs fallback: off = ignore_sysfs_fallback 1
                                     (set for syncboss captures), on = 0 (restore), status = read.
    tools/dev fan-test SECONDS       mainline pwm-fan only: find the fan hwmon by name
                                     (pwmfan, driver pwm-fan), record pwm1_enable/pwm1, set
                                     pwm1=255, sample fan1_input every 2 s for SECONDS (4..60),
                                     then restore both (shell trap: also on error/HUP/TERM).
                                     One self-checked fixed command; the only fan write.
    tools/dev load-step --cores N --seconds S [--fan255 RPM] [--log FILE]
                                     mainline: N (1|2|4|8) busybox `yes` workers on fixed
                                     CPUs, each bounded on the device by `timeout S` (30..300),
                                     under a host-side guard that samples thermal zones, fan and
                                     gauge every 15 s and kills the workers (explicit PIDs) at
                                     >= 78 C, a fan not at 255/rpm above the 70 C trip, gauge
                                     > 42.0 C or < 45 %. --cores 0 = sampling only. Samples are
                                     also appended to FILE (default logs/load-step.log).
    tools/dev drain [--log FILE]     mainline: one gauge drain sample (uptime, capacity, voltage,
                                     current, charge, temp; a fixed read-only command), appended
                                     to FILE (default logs/drain.log) as a `DRAIN epoch=...` line.
                                     If FILE's last sample is younger than DRAIN_GAP (60 s, host
                                     clock), it waits out the rest first: the spacing is the tool's.
    tools/dev heartbeat [--loop SECS] [--max-duration SECS]
                                     mainline init only: refresh the heartbeat dead-man
                                     (one fixed command: target uptime -> /tmp/lvr-heartbeat) and
                                     print the deadline. --loop repeats every SECS (10..300) until
                                     Ctrl-C/kill, the first failure, a reset already armed, or
                                     --max-duration (default 3600 s, max 14400) runs out. When it
                                     stops, the target resets lvr_deadman s after the last heartbeat.
    tools/dev cpufreq-cap cap|uncap|status
                                     cap = policy4/policy7 scaling_max_freq 1478400/1075200,
                                     uncap = 2419200/2841600 (cpuinfo_max). One sha-pinned command:
                                     precheck related_cpus "4 5 6"/"7", driver qcom-cpufreq-hw,
                                     cpuinfo_max (no write on a mismatch), the two literal writes,
                                     readback. status = read-only.
    tools/dev mod-fetch RELEASE --sha SHA256 [--port P]
                                     fetch modules-RELEASE.tar.gz from serve-usb, verify the
                                     pinned sha256 and that every member is under lib/modules/RELEASE/,
                                     unpack into /lib/modules (RAM). RELEASE must equal `uname -r`.
    tools/dev mod-load|mod-unload LABEL --allow FILE
                                     one insmod (with the file's parameters) / rmmod of one
                                     module named in the allowlist FILE (`release REL`, then
                                     `LABEL kernel/<path>.ko sha256:<hex> [param=value ...]
                                     [loaded:MOD] [bound:DEV]`); MOD_DENY never loads.
                                     Format and example: examples/module-allowlist.txt.
    tools/dev kexec-self status|fetch|load|exec|unload [--image-sha S --initrd-sha S --cmdline C]
                                     same-DT self-kexec: fetch Image + initramfs
                                     from the served payload dir (sha-checked), `kexec -s -l` with the
                                     pinned shas and a cmdline from a fixed token allowlist
                                     (rdinit=/init first, panic=0|10, lvr_kexec=1; optional
                                     watchdog.open_timeout=90 initcall_debug ignore_loglevel ...),
                                     exec = arm qcom_wdt (30 s bite) right before
                                     `kexec -e`, refused unless loaded, no NFS/stage 2/load, < 65 C.

Environment: QKX_HOST (default 10.42.0.2) is the headset's address; QUEST_KEXEC_DIR is a
quest-kexec checkout (https://github.com/Mac898/quest-kexec), used only by return-android on
the downstream boot; QUEST_LOG_DIR (default ./logs) is where load-step and drain keep their logs.

Transport: telnet to the unauthenticated root shell the initramfs starts (rootfs/mainline-init and
quest-kexec both do). Storage and MCU interfaces on the headset can be damaged by a stray write, so
every command passes a DENYLIST first (DENY, RISKY and the rule helpers below): anything that could
write persistent storage, program an MCU, read secure memory or reboot the device is refused, and so
are writes into /sys, /proc and configfs and any write naming /sys/power. `tools/dev check 'CMD'` runs
the same test offline. Rebooting only happens through `return-android`. The only write exceptions are
fixed, self-checked strings: `syncboss-txlen`, `fw-fallback`, `fan-test`, `cpufreq-cap`, the `mod-*`
insmod/rmmod commands and `kexec-self`. The denylist guards against slips, it is not a sandbox: it
cannot see through eval or printf-assembled command names. The shell is an interactive ash with line
editing, so every transmitted line stays under LINE_MAX (1000) bytes without control characters;
_send refuses anything else before connecting.
"""
